        "content": "Since the current expansion began in mid-2009, the economy has grown by about 8.3 percent and, as of the first quarter of 2013, real GDP was 3.2 percent above its level at the end of 2007, when the recession began. According to the advance estimate, real GDP advanced 2.5 percent at an annual rate during the first quarter of 2013, marking the 15th straight quarter of growth, after increasing by 0.4 percent in the fourth quarter of last year. The acceleration in growth in the latest quarter reflected faster growth of personal consumption and inventory accumulation, as well as a smaller decline in government spending. These factors helped offset a larger drag from imports and much slower growth in nonresidential fixed investment. Consensus",
